## 2. Independently verified source facts, with pins, scope and design consequences

All captures are exact HTTP response bodies obtained via `public_https_get` on 2026-10-06 (UTC timestamps in §5). Capture identity = sha256 of the body bytes; bytes are stored by the harness under `public_captures/<sha256>.body`.

### F1. libsndfile's supported-format matrix is pinned and versioned — usable as the honest I/O boundary backbone **[SUPPORTED, EXECUTED-C]**
Source: `https://libsndfile.github.io/libsndfile/formats.html` (capture sha256 `80e0e3844e962a4fc70954036f036ad42cf5e0b0c586aed8da4ec0e60255487b`, page footer states "The libsndfile version: 1.2.2", `Last-Modified: Tue, 01 Sep 2026`).
Facts read directly from the table: WAV, AIFF/AIFC, AU/SND, RAW, W64, CAF, SD2, FLAC and others are supported; per-encoding read/write for unsigned/signed 8-bit PCM, 16/24/32-bit PCM, 32-bit float, 64-bit double, µ-law, A-law, IMA ADPCM, MS ADPCM, GSM 6.10 (coverage varies per container). Text below the table: FLAC and Ogg/Vorbis read/write **from version 1.0.18**; Ogg/Opus **from 1.0.29**; **MP3 read/write from version 1.1.0**.
Consequence: a proposal can give a concrete supported input boundary (WAV/AIFF/FLAC/Opus/MP3 + the listed PCM encodings, via libsndfile ≥ 1.1.0 for MP3) instead of hand-waving "every codec". Equally important: **MP4/M4A/AAC are not listed on this page** — do not claim them; that is exactly the brief's "practical supported input/output boundary".
Limit: the page is vendor documentation of *intent*; it does not prove any particular installed build has these features (MP3 additionally depends on build-time mpg123/LAME, see F4). The visible table does **not** mention RF64 — see C2.

### F2. libsamplerate's streaming contract is pinned — this is the frame-exact cue-mapping foundation **[SUPPORTED, EXECUTED-C]**
Source: `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/libsndfile/libsamplerate/master/docs/api_full.md` (capture sha256 `0731920a8b1ce535a8222e794677cf342ba3ffac36e3522dfcda47340f592a33`, repo path `docs/api_full.md`, branch `master`).
Facts read directly: `src_process(state, SRC_DATA*)` is the streaming API, callable repeatedly; `SRC_DATA` fields `data_in/data_out`, `input_frames/output_frames` (caller-filled), `input_frames_used/output_frames_gen` (library-filled per call), `end_of_input` (0 while more input remains, 1 otherwise), `src_ratio = output_rate / input_rate`; **`data_in` and `data_out` may not overlap** (error otherwise); `src_reset` "should be called whenever a sample rate converter is to be used on two separate, unrelated pieces of audio"; `src_clone` copies converter state so conversion can later resume at a specific stream location with identical output; `src_set_ratio` bypasses the library's **smooth transition** between successive ratios and gives a step change.
Consequences (design-level, my inferences from these pinned facts):
1. A per-clip resampler must use `input_frames_used`/`output_frames_gen` as the only truth for position bookkeeping; a proposal that computes clip-local offsets by arithmetic alone (in×ratio) is asserting behavior the library does not promise per call.
2. Resampler state must never straddle a clip join: `src_reset` at every new (source, region) pair, per the vendor's own instruction — otherwise tail samples of one region prime the next and exported cue positions drift by filter length.
3. Ratio changes at edit boundaries require `src_set_ratio` (documented step) or a fresh state; relying on `SRC_DATA.src_ratio` alone produces an undocumented glide across the boundary.
4. `src_clone` gives a clean primitive for scrub/preview-vs-export determinism: export can replay from a cloned state to guarantee the same samples as preview.

