### F6. A real, measured, still-open upstream defect showing exactly the "silent cue shift" hazard the brief warns about **[SUPPORTED as documented claim; severity is my inference]**
Source: audacity/audacity PR `#11963` "Add video playback" (captured via GitHub search API, capture sha256 `5e2e9fb8b803ec261d9ba3bc31fe4abb0351c03701d8263a47ba2aded26fb1c0`, truncated; the PR object — open, created 2026-09-05, updated 2026-09-11 — and its body were in the captured bytes). The body states, verbatim in substance: `FFmpegImportFileHandle::Import` converts `AVStream::start_time` "as though its time base were always microseconds. On MPEG-TS this leaves picture and sound about 153 ms apart, and about 21 ms on AAC. Correcting it changes where every AAC, MPEG-TS and LAME MP3 import lands on the timeline," and the author deliberately left it out of scope, measured and documented in `tools/videosync/README.md`.
**Causal limits:** this is a contributor's disclosure inside an open PR (open as of capture date), not an upstream-accepted fix with a merged regression test — so it does NOT satisfy a "followed through fix + regression test" chain on its own; I am offering F4 as the completed chain and F6 as the consequential hazard evidence. It is also about Audacity's FFmpeg import path, which is a *different component* from libsndfile (F4) — the two do not corroborate each other numerically.
**Design consequence (strong, and the reason I include it):** "where does t=0 of an imported file sit" is a real, recurring, silently-wrong class of bug in shipping editors. A proposal must (a) define project time as anchored to the first sample of the decoded stream, (b) record in per-source metadata any container start offset it chose to ignore, and (c) treat any import library's start-time semantics as untrusted until measured. A single successful import example proves nothing here — the Audacity disclosure shows a defect that survived years of production use.

